#=GF RT   Mechanism of 5' splice site transfer for human spliceosome activation.
#=GF RA   Charenton C, Wilkinson ME, Nagai K
#=GF RL   Science. 2019;364:362-367.
#=GF CC   U4 small nuclear RNA (U4 snRNA) is a component of the major U2-dependent
#=GF CC   spliceosome. It forms a duplex with U6, and with each splicing round, it
#=GF CC   is displaced from U6 (and the spliceosome) in an ATP-dependent manner,
#=GF CC   allowing U6 to re-fold and create the active site for splicing catalysis.
#=GF CC   A recycling process involving protein Prp24 re-anneals U4 and U6. U4/U6
#=GF CC   di-snRNA is conserved in eukaryotes and is part of the large
#=GF CC   ribonucleoprotein complex: U4 / U6.U5 tri-snRNP [5,8]. The structure of U4
#=GF CC   snRNA from yeast[5,6,8,9,11] and human[2,7,10,12,13] has been reported. U4
#=GF CC   snRNA present two regions where U4 and U6 form helixes (stem II and stem I
#=GF CC   share helixes) and two stem loops (5p-SL and 3p-SL), in human, is also
#=GF CC   present an extra SL in the 3p end. U4 snRNA presents a k-turn RNA motif in
#=GF CC   the 5p-SL related to recognition of Snu13 protein [8]. The structure of U4
#=GF CC   spliceosomal RNA has been reported in PDBs: 2N7M, 3JCM, 3JCR, 5GAN, 5GAP,
#=GF CC   5NRL, 5O9Z, 5ZWM, 5ZWO, 6AH0, 6AHD, AQW6, 6QX9 [5-13]
#=GF WK   U4_spliceosomal_RNA
#=GF SQ   177

